(logs)
  > (item)
    (author) Søren Kierkegaard · written as "A", edited by Victor Eremita
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-09-16
    (link) https://tekster.kb.dk/text/sks-ee1-txt-root
    (rating) 5
    (slug) marry-and-you-will-regret-it
    (title) ""Marry, and you will regret it; do not marry, you will also regret it.""
    (updated) 2026-09-16
    (description) The most-quoted sentence in Either/Or belongs to "A", the aesthete of Part I, printed under the pseudonym Victor Eremita and headed with the book's own title — "Enten – Eller. Et exstatisk Foredrag." Kierkegaard wrote it, then asked in print, in the 1846 First and Last Declaration, that anyone quoting the pseudonymous books cite the pseudonym and not him; the entire quotation trade does the opposite. Restores the three clauses the internet drops — including "hang yourself, you will regret it," which extends regret past the point where anyone is left to feel it and gives the game away: this was never a claim about outcomes. Tracks the translation drift from Leve-Viisdom to "all philosophy," and shows why Judge William's two letters in Part II make the lecture a symptom rather than a verdict.
  > (item)
    (author) J. J. van der Leeuw · misattributed to Søren Kierkegaard
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-09-04
    (link) https://selfdefinition.org/van-der-leeuw/J.J.-van-der-Leeuw-Conquest-Of-Illusion.pdf
    (rating) 5
    (slug) life-is-not-a-problem-to-be-solved
    (title) ""Life is not a problem to be solved, but a reality to be experienced.""
    (updated) 2026-09-04
    (description) Kierkegaard never wrote it. The line is J. J. van der Leeuw's, from The Conquest of Illusion (1928) — verified against the book — and the original opens "The mystery of life," three words whose deletion changes the claim from a boundary on what analysis can reach into a general licence to stop thinking. Traces the misattribution through a 1948 journal calling it "the famous Kierkegaardian slogan" (an adjective of resemblance) to a 1976 book printing it as his (an adjective of authorship), and asks why it stuck: because Kierkegaard really did think no system can contain an existing person — he just got there by fear and trembling rather than cosmic consciousness.
  > (item)
    (author) Henri Pirenne
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-07-19
    (link) https://archive.org/stream/medievalcitiesth00pire_0/medievalcitiesth00pire_0_djvu.txt
    (rating) 5
    (slug) charlemagne-without-muhammad
    (title) ""Without Islam, the Frankish Empire would probably never have existed and Charlemagne, without Mahomet, would be inconceivable.""
    (updated) 2026-07-19
    (description) Henri Pirenne's counterfactual — near-universally credited to Mohammed and Charlemagne (1937) but actually from Medieval Cities (1925), where the text reads "Mahomet." The claim isn't that Islam built Europe; it's that Islam removed the alternative, closing a Mediterranean the West would otherwise have kept facing. Laid out in full: the argument, the century of attacks on it (Dennett, Lombard, Bolin, Hodges & Whitehouse, Wickham, Effros), what survived, and why a counterfactual can't be settled by evidence.
  > (item)
    (author) Edward Albee
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-07-18
    (link) https://edwardalbeesociety.org/works/tiny-alice/
    (rating) 5
    (slug) every-monster-was-a-man-first
    (title) ""Every monster was a man first.""
    (updated) 2026-07-18
    (description) A line from Edward Albee's Tiny Alice (1964) that the internet has flattened into a compassion slogan. Restored to a play about an innocent dismantled by polite, well-dressed people, it reads colder: monstrousness isn't a separate species but a destination, and the load-bearing word is "first" — there was a man, then there was a monster, and Albee never tells you what happened in between.
  > (item)
    (author) Socrates · Ben Jonson
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-06-11
    (link)
    (rating) 5
    (slug) speak-that-i-may-see-thee
    (title) ""Speak, that I may see thee.""
    (updated) 2026-06-11
    (description) An ancient line attributed to Socrates — recorded by Apuleius, passed down through Petrarch and Erasmus, and given its famous English form by Ben Jonson in Timber: or, Discoveries (1641): "Language most shows a man; speak that I may see thee." The idea: a face shows a body, but speech shows a mind — a person stays invisible until they talk.
  > (item)
    (author) Herbert A. Simon
    (category) Quote
    (date) 2026-06-07
    (link)
    (rating) 5
    (slug) poverty-of-attention
    (title) ""A wealth of information creates a poverty of attention.""
    (updated) 2026-06-07
    (description) Herbert Simon's 1971 line — the origin of "the attention economy." Once information becomes abundant, it stops being the scarce resource; the new bottleneck is attention, the finite thing information consumes. So the real skill shifts from finding information to choosing what to ignore.
